{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\nA full `make verify` on macOS/arm64 (`PYTEST_WORKERS=4`, 2026-11-16, host load average 40-70 on 18 CPUs, with other sessions' verifies running) ended `4 failed, 21291 passed, 29 skipped in 10796.46s (2:59:56)`. All 4 failures raised `subprocess.TimeoutExpired: ... observability.sh ... timed out after 60 seconds` from `run_observability(..., timeout=60)`:\n- tests/scripts/test_observability_reviewbot_webhook_auth.py::test_reviewbot_webhook_auth_rejections_publish_from_health_payload\n- tests/scripts/test_observability_rotation_2dc7.py::test_shrinking_journal_publishes_zero_not_the_remaining_total[replication_errors-REPL_OFFSET_FILE] and [voter_errors-VOTER_OFFSET_FILE]\n- tests/scripts/test_observability_rotation_2dc7.py::test_markers_after_a_rotation_still_publish\n\nThe same files alone afterwards: `10 passed in 89.99s`. A third file (test_observability_interrupt_reasons.py) failed the same way in another session.\n\n## Root cause (measured 2026-11-17 PST; full evidence in the 09:5x PST comment)\n- No hang is involved. A stubbed observability.sh run spawns about 480 short-lived processes (exec counts by PATH shims). Its wall time scales with process count times CPU contention.\n- Idle cost: one run takes 3-8 s wall and 3-3.5 s CPU at load 30.\n- Under the suite: the failing default tier ran at about 7-8x its documented 22-26 min.\n- Contention reproduction: 24 concurrent runs under `taskpolicy -c background`. With the original stubs, 24 of 24 exceeded 60 s. With cheaper stubs, 24 of 24 still did. With every stub removed, the runs took 42-47 s, about 75% of the bound.\n- So reducing per-run work cannot keep a fixed 60 s bound clear of contention. The bound is a hang guard, and it sits inside the range of legitimate run times.\n\n## Approach (operator decision, 2026-11-17)\nOperator approved on 2026-11-17 after comparing CPython (test.support LOOPBACK/SHORT/LONG_TIMEOUT), Bazel (test size timeouts), Go (-timeout), LLVM lit and pytest-timeout practice: replace the hard-coded 60 s subprocess bound with a named, measured timeout tier.\n- Add one constant, `SCRIPT_RUN_TIMEOUT = 135.0`, in `tests/_observability_harness.py`, the single source for every whole-script observability run. Its derivation comment records:\n  - Normal loaded run: idle 3.5-4 s times the measured 7-8x full-suite slowdown, about 27 s.\n  - 5x that is about 135 s. That clears the 42-47 s worst stub-free contention by about 3x, so a starved run passes.\n  - A real hang still fails in 135 s rather than at pytest's 300 s budget.\n  - A test that runs the script twice spends at most 270 s, under the 300 s per-test budget (pyproject.toml `timeout = 300`), with 30 s left for setup and teardown.\n- The scale lives in code. There is no environment variable, config key or CLI flag.\n- Replace every literal `timeout=60` that bounds an observability script run with `timeout=SCRIPT_RUN_TIMEOUT`. That is 32 sites across 16 files under tests/scripts/ (`grep -rn \"timeout=60\" tests/scripts/test_observability_*.py` lists 33; Serena finds no further references because callers pass a literal):\n  - `run_observability` calls;\n  - the `--report-exit` runs and the census-harness `subprocess.run` calls in test_observability_timeout_9313.py;\n  - the installer run in test_observability_journal_bounding_1205.py:324.\n- Excluded: the one `timeout=60` inside the sample-source string in test_observability_harness_isolation.py:194. That is text the anti-vacuity scan reads, not a bound.\n- Out of scope: the deliberate short bound (`timeout=3`, harness reaping test), and the existing 120/180/300 s bounds, which already exceed 60 s.\n- Hang detection is kept. `run_observability` still raises `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` naming the command and the bound, after reaping the process group.\n\n## Scope\n- `tests/_observability_harness.py`: the `SCRIPT_RUN_TIMEOUT` constant and its derivation comment.\n- The 16 `tests/scripts/test_observability_*.py` files listed above: replace the literal 60 s bounds with the constant (imported from `_observability_harness`).\n- `tests/scripts/test_observability_harness_isolation.py`: 2 regression tests.\n  - An AST scan of `tests/scripts/test_observability_*.py` fails on any call with a literal `timeout=60` keyword. It skips string contents, because a string is not a call.\n  - A check that `2 * SCRIPT_RUN_TIMEOUT` is below the pytest `timeout` ini value read from pyproject.toml.\n\n## Testing\n- Failure path: the new AST-scan test fails on origin/main (32 literal sites) and passes after the change.\n- The budget check passes (270 < 300). It fails if the tier is raised past 150 s.\n- Hang path: the existing `timeout=3` reaping test in test_observability_harness_isolation.py still passes, showing that `run_observability` still raises `TimeoutExpired` on a stalled run.\n- Contention re-check, recorded on this ticket: the scratch K=24 concurrent `taskpolicy -c background` run of the rotation-test environment is repeated with the 135 s bound. It must show 0 of 24 timeouts.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The cost driver of a stubbed observability.sh run is measured and recorded on this ticket.\n- [ ] The 4 tests pass with the cause addressed at class level: the bound is a named tier derived from measured durations, applied to every 60 s observability script bound, with no retries. Checked by the new AST scan and the budget check, plus the recorded K=24 contention re-check.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794950482883696672,"uuid":"a465ed75-bd4c-4264-bd7d-058ce1469335"}